   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to the definition of \"small business\"; and to amend chapter 568 of the laws of 2015, amending the state finance law relating to requiring state agencies to pay small businesses within fifteen days of receipt of an invoice, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7993",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7993-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §179-f, St Fin L; amd §2, Chap 568 of 2015",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7993\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the state finance law, in relation\nto the definition of \"small business\"; and to amend chapter 568 of the\nlaws of 2015, amending the state finance law relating to requiring\nstate agencies to pay small businesses within fifteen days of receipt\nof an invoice, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend chapter 568 of the laws of 2015\nin order to implement an effective system to ensure that small\nbusinesses are paid within fifteen days of an agency receiving an\ninvoice for certain payments so that it may continue to sustain itself\nand conduct its business activities.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 6 of section 179-f of the state finance\nlaw as amended by chapter 36 of the laws of 2016 to clarify that a\nsmall business will be paid by agencies within 15 days of receipt of\nan invoice submitted electronically in conformance with the procedures\nof the statewide financial system, absent a showing by the contracting\nagency of circumstances beyond its control. Section 1 also amends\nsubdivision 6 of section 179-f of the state finance law to amend the\ndefinition of \"small business\" to mean businesses that employ no more\nthan 200 employees, rather than 300, and to provide that small\nbusinesses must upon request submit sufficient documentation to\nconfirm its status as a small business.\n\nSection 2 amends the effective date of chapter 568 of the laws of 2015\nto provide for such laws effectiveness 365 days after it shall have\nbecome a law, March 31,2017, and shall apply only to invoices received\nafter such date.\n\nSection 3 provides for the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis bill reflects the agreed upon amendments to chapter 586 of the\nlaws of 2015. (See approval memo 24 of 2015).\n\nMany small businesses have limited available funds to carry them from\neach respective pay period, let alone month to month. Therefore, in\norder to ensure that small businesses and their employees are given\nthe best opportunity to succeed and sustain themselves, it is\nimperative to implement an accelerated prompt pay system to ensure the\nsustainability of these businesses. Shortening the prompt payment\nprovisions of the state finance law from 30 days to 15 days will help\nto facilitate meaningful and lasting participation of small businesses\non state contracts.\n\nGiven the advances the state has made with improving its business\npractices and processing of payments through the Statewide Financial\nSystem and other practices, the provisions of this bill will be able\nto be implemented smoothly in order to help small businesses succeed.\nSimilar provisions have been successfully implemented in other parts\nof the country.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is new legislation.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nMinimal.\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as\na chapter of laws of 2015 amending the state finance law related to\nrequiring state agencies to pay small businesses within fifteen days\nof receipt of an invoice, as amended, takes effect.\n",
